#show: nsf-doc.with(page-limit: 1, draft: DRAFT, title: [Synergistic Activities — Jonathan D. Matthis])

+ *Development and open distribution of accessible research tooling.*
  Created and maintains FreeMoCap, a free, open-source markerless motion
  capture platform, and deliberately architected it as a suite of modular,
  independently documented repositories (camera synchronization, pose
  estimation, data processing, visualization) so that researchers can
  understand, deploy, and extend individual pipeline stages in their own
  projects rather than adopting an opaque monolith.

+ *Technical training and community support at scale.* Provides extensive
  informal training and technical support to a community of several thousand
  students, early-career researchers, and professionals — including guidance
  on conducting and publishing their work in independent, publicly visible
  ways — through community channels, written documentation, and regular
  live tutorial streams.

+ *Public, reproducible research-software development.* Conducts the entire
  development process "in public": all work is hosted openly on GitHub with
  intentional documentation of both the technical pipeline and the
  organizational processes behind it, explicitly to make it easier for other
  groups to follow the same open, reproducible development model.

+ *Contributions to the broader open-source and research community.*
  Regularly contributes code review, pull requests, and technical guidance
  to third-party open-source projects, and provides public technical
  feedback to researchers presenting motion-capture and computer-vision
  methods work outside traditional review venues.

+ *Public science communication.* Produces publicly available video content
  (YouTube tutorials and Twitch live streams) on motion capture methods and
  open-source scientific software, and has discussed accessible research
  tooling and open science in podcast appearances.
